<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's Sunny. I'm Happy! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> This Beautiful Day</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Sunny. I'm Happy!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create descriptive captions for images with simple words. Interactive: Write short role plays to be acted out based on class content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can create descriptive captions for weather images using simple words (e.g., "It's sunny."). Can write and perform short role plays on weather and school. (e.g., "It's sunny. Let's play!" "Yes, let's go!"). Can write and organize simple texts about weather and feelings (e.g., "I am sad on rainy days.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activate prior knowledge of weather and feelings vocabulary, setting the stage for writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of large flashcards depicting different weather conditions (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and corresponding happy/sad faces. Students say the weather and how they feel. Example: Teacher shows a "sunny" flashcard and says, "It's sunny! How do you feel?" Students respond chorally or individually: "I'm happy!" Teacher then asks, "How do you spell 'sunny'?" and points to letters on the board if needed, guiding towards writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple captions on the board for one or two flashcards. For example, for the "sunny" flashcard, the teacher writes "It's sunny." and then "I'm happy." The teacher points to 'It's' and says, "This is 'It is'. We use 'It's' because it's faster!" (/s/ sound in "sunny"). The teacher then asks, "What do you see?" and writes students' suggestions, emphasizing simple sentences for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we write about in our sentences? (Weather and feelings)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'I'm' a long word or a short word? (Short) What two words make 'I'm'? (I am)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "This Beautiful Day." Focus on example written texts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short projected slide or handout with 3-4 simple picture-sentence captions. Each picture shows a different weather condition (e.g., a child with an umbrella for "It's rainy. I'm sad."; children playing in the sun for "It's sunny. We're happy!"; a windy day for "It's windy. I'm bored."). The texts use contractions and present simple tense.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing emotions and describing weather using simple written sentences and contractions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to each picture and reads the caption aloud, clearly pronouncing /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy." Students repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students draw a line from an emoji (happy, sad, bored) to the sentence that describes that feeling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identify:</b> Teacher asks: "Which picture says 'It's rainy'?" Students point to the correct picture.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing. Students practice forming simple descriptive sentences and short role-play lines.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing descriptive sentences about weather and feelings, using contractions (I'm, it's) and present simple. Vocabulary: sun, rain, cloud, windy, happy, sad, bored, excited, enjoy, play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with 4-5 images of different weather conditions and a sentence starter below each. They complete the sentences using given word banks.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: Picture of sunny day. Sentence: "It's _____. I'm _____." (Word bank: sunny, happy)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: Picture of rainy day. Sentence: "It's _____. I feel _____." (Word bank: rainy, sad)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher walks around, providing help with spelling and grammar (e.g., ensuring contractions are used).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Caption Writing with Word Box (Semi-controlled):</b> Students are given 3-4 new weather images without sentence starters, but with a word box containing key vocabulary (e.g., "sunny, rainy, cloud, happy, sad, play, enjoy"). They write two simple sentences (a caption) for each image describing the weather and a feeling or activity.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: Picture of children playing in the park on a sunny day. Students write: "It's sunny. I'm happy. I play."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reminds students to use contractions like "It's" and "I'm". Phonemic awareness: teacher highlights /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy" while checking their writing.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Pair Caption Check:</b> Students work in pairs. They exchange their completed worksheets from Activity 2 and review each other's captions. They check for correct vocabulary, simple sentence structure, and legible writing. The teacher provides a simple checklist: "Did you use 'It's' or 'I'm'?", "Is the weather word correct?", "Is the feeling word correct?", "Is your writing clear?".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "This Beautiful Day." Focus on fluency and collaborative writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative writing of short descriptive captions and a mini role-play based on chosen weather and feelings.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups of 3. Each group chooses one large weather image (e.g., a bright sunny beach, a cloudy day at school, a windy playground).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1: Caption Creation:</b> For their chosen image, the group collaboratively writes 2-3 descriptive captions, similar to those practiced in Stage 3. They focus on describing the weather and how people/they feel, and what they might do. (e.g., for a sunny beach: "It's sunny and warm. I'm excited! Let's play in the sun!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2: Mini Role-Play Writing:</b> Based on their chosen weather image and captions, the group then writes a very short, 2-3 line role-play. This role-play should incorporate a WH-question ("How do you feel today?") and express feelings politely.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example for a sunny day:
                                <p style="margin: 4px 0; padding-left: 15px;">Student A: "It's sunny! How do you feel today?"</p>
                                <p style="margin: 4px 0; padding-left: 15px;">Student B: "I feel happy, thank you! Let's play outside!"</p>
                                <p style="margin: 4px 0; padding-left: 15px;">Student C: "Yes, let's go!"</p>
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, offering support with vocabulary and sentence structure, encouraging the use of contractions ('I'm', 'it's') and polite phrases. Teacher also reminds students to think about the blending of sounds for words like "rain" and "rainy" when writing.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then briefly share their written captions and perform their short role-plays to the class.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of writing skills and language use.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and a checklist for the written captions and role-play scripts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their collaboratively written captions and short role-play scripts. The teacher collects them to assess individual and group writing abilities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content (Captions):</b> Use of target vocabulary (weather, feelings) and adjectives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar (Captions & Role-play):</b> Correct use of "It's" and "I'm," and present simple tense. Correct formation of WH-questions ("How do you feel?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Organization:</b> Captions clearly describe the image. Role-play is coherent and easy to understand.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Legibility:</b> Written work is neat and readable.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic Use:</b> Use of polite phrases (e.g., "thank you" in role-play).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new weather word you wrote today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write a sentence about how I feel: (e.g., I'm happy.)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and look ahead, integrating learnings.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing today was easy for me?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing today was hard? What word do I need to practice writing?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What aspects of the writing activities worked well for 3rd graders? Did they successfully use contractions and basic sentence structures?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did students face in creating their captions or role-plays? Were there common errors in vocabulary or grammar? Were phonemic awareness activities helpful for spelling?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I adjust or scaffold future writing tasks to better support their development?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing lesson prepares students to consolidate and summarize information about weather and feelings in Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), where they will use their learned vocabulary and sentence structures to create a small project or presentation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>